TITLE: SeqNMA: sequential network meta-analysis with monitoring boundaries â€” a methods and software demonstration on a synthetic network
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: Cumulative NMA z-score vs O'Brien-Fleming monitoring boundary
DATA: A synthetic, reproducibly seeded 12-study network of four treatments (A, B, C, D), simulated on the log-odds-ratio scale; no real patient or trial data.
PATH: C:\Projects\seqnma-e156
OJS: https://synthesis-medicine.org/index.php/journal/article/view/seqnma-e156
      sub=146 pub=297 PDF galley=510 JATS galley=511 | Synthesis J1 | Tool Paper | Issue 9 Jun 2026

CURRENT BODY (156 words):
Can trial sequential analysis extend to network meta-analysis, giving formal stopping rules as evidence accumulates across a treatment network? We demonstrate on a synthetic, reproducibly seeded example: twelve chronologically ordered studies reporting pairwise comparisons among four treatments. An in-browser engine rebuilds a cumulative contrast-based NMA at each addition, computing required information from the heterogeneity-adjusted diversity design effect DÂ². The estimand, the cumulative NMA z-score against the Oâ€™Brien-Fleming boundary (z_k=z_Î±/âˆš(information fraction)) Bonferroni-corrected across six comparisons, shows A-versus-C crossing after study 10 while A-versus-D inconclusive at 66 percent of required information. Futility boundaries are non-binding by convention; the boundary mathematics (diversity adjustment, required information, spending function) were independently re-derived and numerically confirmed. Crucially, the method assumes but does not test consistency, with no design-by-treatment interaction or node-splitting, so crossings hold only if direct and indirect evidence agree. The twelve-study example is synthetic and cannot support any clinical claim, a software demonstration, not an empirical finding.

